Date: Tue, 19 Jan 93 11:43:41 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: Re:  PPL: Restitution is Usually Not Enough

I asked Mark:
> If you're willing to include investigation, legal and court costs,
> why not also just add in the cost of insuring against the
> possibility of not catching and convicting the criminal?  That's
> what you're trying to with your community fund idea, but why
> socialize it that much?...
 
Mark Pickens responded:
>Legal and related costs can be logically connected to that particular crime.
>Insurance costs can not.  For all we know, that one crime was the only one
>committed by that criminal.  
>As for socialization, if it's useful and all parties agree to it , why not?

Tom Morrow similarly wrote:
>Including insurance costs would bridge the gap, but I believe there
>are moral grounds to object to forcing a caught criminal to effectively pay
>for the risks imposed by other, unapprehended criminals.

I think such insurance costs can be directly laid at the feet of the
criminal, and are not paying for risks imposed by other criminals.
The rule can be that *if* the criminal turns themselves in within some
standard short time, or can credibly argue that they were trying to
do so as soon as possible but were delayed, then no such insurance
costs need be paid.  However, if the criminal did not turn themselves
in, then they are imposing on the victim the risk of not getting paid,
risk which the victim can insure against without reference to any
other criminals or crimes.  Thus I don't think there is a conflict
with non-coercion and requiring extra payment for this insurance.  

With full and certain restitution, the point of legal credit is
greatly diminished.  I don't need to avoid folks with criminal records
if I don't care whether crimes happen.  

Robin Hanson

