I read Mark Desilets's post today about wanting scientific evidence
that magickal/yogic rituals work and was inspired to write the
following, which I sent first to Arcana, a mailing list devoted to
scholarly study of the occult. Since I know that there was a
discussion about Aleister Crowley on Extropians shortly before I
signed up most recently, I thought this might be relevant here too. If
_you_ don't think it's relevant, don't clutter the list; send flames
directly to me. Otherwise, open discussion is welcome.

P.S. At Arisia, a friend told me he had seen a button with my .sig on
it. I then remembered that Nancy Lebovitz, fandom's most prolific
buttonmaker, had seen my .sig on sci.cryonics and said she was going
to "steal" it. I tromped up to her room and demanded a free button
with my slogan on it, which I got. Another blow for immortalism (the
button, that is)!

>This past weekend I attended Arisia '93, a science fiction convention
>in Boston. At the con there were three scheduled workshops/discussion
>groups on magick and two on Tarot. The first magickal discussion was
>about the question of whether magick exists, a question which I'm sure
>would cause some controversy even among Arcanans. The two speakers
>were a Mr. d'Gryphon, a magick practicioner and teacher, and John
>Norman, author of the "Gor" series of SF/F, SM/M novels. The latter
>was surprised that he had been invited to speak since he had little to
>do with magickal issues apart from being a skeptic on the topic.
>Norman (a surprisingly personable chap for someone who reportedly
>writes novels in which the preferred mode of sexual interaction is
>rape) claimed that magick had to be experimentally demonstrable in a
>laboratory before anyone could justifiably claim it existed. 
>
>At this point I objected from the audience that I believed that magick
>worked synchronistically, and that synchronicity could not be tested
>in the laboratory, since science would not recognise "coincidence" as
>a valid means of obtaining results. As an example I gave the possibly
>apocryphal story of the magician who cast a spell for a large sum of
>money without limiting the means by which he could attain it and a
>short time later received an appropriate amount of money when a
>beloved relative died in an accident. Norman answered that
>synchronistic magic could be tested in the laboratory by casting a
>spell for a desired result and seeing whether the result is attained
>within a specific amount of time. This gave me something to think
>about, so I shut up.
>
>After the panel, though, I thought of several objections to Norman's
>answer. First, this answer leaves no room for the irony apparent in so
>many synchronistic phenomena, an irony which often seems to imply an
>intentionality, the same intentionality that led Jung to classify
>synchronicity as a psychic phenomenon (as opposed to his predecessor
>Kammerer, who thought "seriality," as he called synchronicity, was
>merely a physical law). What if, I thought, I cast a spell to obtain a
>horse within a month, and someone gave me a horse a day after the
>month had elapsed? Or I inherited a very expensive painting of a horse
>within the time period? Or my spouse developed throat cancer suddenly
>and became very hoarse? All these would be classified as failures
>within a scientific framework but might be seen as significant effects
>(perhaps backfiring) of the spell within a magickal context.
>
>As an example of the irony often inherent in synchronicity I will
>relate the following true story. When I was in college, I was part of
>a club that played the game Nomic. The relevant feature of our game
>was the rule which said that under certain circumstances, players had
>to deliver one-minute impromptu speeches about topics randomly chosen
>from a pile of 3x5" index cards previously generated by the players.
>(Included were topics from random scribbles and nonsense words to
>themes like "Why Vampires Like Cream Cheese" and "Commies from Mars.")
>Since I was moving and the club was breaking up, we decided to have a
>party where we would deliver all the remaining speeches in one evening
>(there must have been seventy in the pile at the time). The players in
>the club were for the most part aware of my interest in synchronicity,
>and there was one player, Keith, who was particularly skeptical about
>it. I don't remember what the last topic of the evening was, or who
>gave the last speech, but Keith had the second-to-last speech, and the
>topic on his card was "The Last Word." "So much for synchronicity,"
>said Keith. I sighed wistfully. It wasn't until later that I realised
>Keith had gotten what he wanted: the last word.
>
>So synchronicity often has this whimsical, ironic nature that would be
>very hard to measure in a laboratory. Another objection I thought of
>to Norman's answer, but one which may not present ultimately as much
>of an obstacle, is that some results may be harder to obtain than
>others and some practicioners may be less skilled or powerful than
>others, and so a negative result in the laboratory may just be
>evidence that results were not obtainable under the current
>conditions. At the moment, I'm not sure we have any way to control for
>these variables.
>
>So there you have it, a few meditations on what I consider Crowley's
>invaluable dictum 
