I must not be being clear enough.  Here's the scenario.  I claim "I've
been robbed!".  The criminal has a week to turn themselves in.  If
still no criminal then, I then seek the protection/insurance company
that will pay me the $1000 restitution due, while visiting the least
punishment on the criminal (a combination of fine due if caught and
probability of catching them).  The fine that the criminal would pay here
would then be due to costs of restitution, chasing, prosecution, and
insurance in case they aren't caught.  No other crime or criminal is
effected here.  

The amount the protection/insurance company will charge will depend on
many things, perhaps including my past checking of credit (though I
can't see that being very relevant to probability of catching given a
crime).  But I don't care.  It's the criminal who has to pay any
increased insurance costs from their being expected to be harder to
catch, not me.  

