Date: Tue, 19 Jan 93 18:24:46 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: PPL: Restitution is Usually Not Enough

Continuing our exploration of the sublties of restitution, Robin Hanson
appears to have suggested that criminals could justly be forced to pay for
their victims' insurance costs if they failed to turn themselves in within
some particular deadline.

>Thus I don't think there is a conflict
>with non-coercion and requiring extra payment for this insurance.  

I am not sure this solves the moral problem of having the criminal pay for
the costs imposed by third party criminals.  If I understand Robin's
argument, he is saying that this problem is obviated by offering the
criminal a chance to turn herself in and avoid the extra charge.  But this
justification alone will not suffice.  We could also say that jaywalkers
will be executed unless they turn themselves in.  But executing a
recalcitrant jaywalker is clearly unjust.  

The problem remains:  insurance costs reflect the risks imposed by all
criminals--not just those who are caught.  Forcing only those who are
caught to pay for the victims' insurance demands more than mere
restitution, since the caught criminal is forced to pay for others' crimes.
 (There may be an argument for forcing the criminal to pay for any
*increase* in insurance rates that result from her crimes, however.)

In a later message, Robin said:
>The amount the protection/insurance company will charge will depend on
>many things, perhaps including my past checking of credit (though I
>can't see that being very relevant to probability of catching given a
>crime).  

I agree that a legal credit check will do little to increase the
probability of catching a criminal after she has acted.  It's too late by
then.  But it should help to prevent the crime from ever happening.  And
insurance companies care about avoiding claims on their policies, not just
finding someone to pay for them.

>But I don't care.  It's the criminal who has to pay any
>increased insurance costs from their being expected to be harder to
>catch, not me.  

But if, as will surely often be the case, criminals are not always caught
or able to pay restitution, the insurance company will take the loss.  And
it will pass this loss on to its customers.   So victims *will* pay for
criminals who are harder to catch--unless, of course, you force the (even
smaller) percentage of caught and solvent criminals to pay for the costs
imposed by their more elusive and insolvent competitors.  It looks as if
this sort of argument will lead to fewer and fewer criminals being milked
for more and more money.  I suspect that honest consumers would end up
footing the bill (but can't be sure, of course, how things would actually
work out in practice).  In any case, I still doubt that forcing caught
criminals to bear all their victims' insurance costs is morally equivalent
to restitution.

Maybe I've misunderstood Robin, though.  If so, I'm confident I'll hear
