Date: Tue, 19 Jan 93 21:03:25 -0500
From: James E Hoburg <hoburg@ramsesii.ultryx.com>
Subject: Re: CORP: Shareholders etc

Krzys' writes:
 > When is it then justifiable to kill an unarmed person who is not
 > initiating force? What set of sophisticated meta-rules will justify 
 > that? When is it OK to rob others with all force necessary to rob
 > if we fail to make enough on our own to have three VCRs and must
 > put up with one, frustrated with envy?
 > What are the sophisticated meta-rules that justify this?

To answer your questions:  Maybe never.  Maybe none.  Maybe never.
Maybe none.  I wouldn't (maybe) wish to underwrite a society where the answers
were otherwise.  But more about that 1st question later.  In the meantime,
read on.

With my earlier blather about meta-rules I was attempting to get at the follow-
ing.  My apologies for not making myself clear.

  *  Societies/Gov'ts. are organized on some set of underlying rules.

  *  Some sets of rules may result in "undesirable" outcomes.  For example, if
     I am a serf in a some feudal monarchic culture, I probably won't consider
     the outcome of the rules which result in such a state desirable.  I may
     feel that the privileged nobility will have first dibs on distribution
     of resources including the fruits of my labor.  I may feel this is not an
     equitable state of affairs.  I may feel at a permanent disadvantage because
     surname or family tree determine who gets the goodies.

     I have no in-system recourse to change the operant rules from "the nobility
     gets the best" to "hoburg gets some goodies, too."  Not even if I am an
     enterprising capitalist in potential.  My input just doesn't count.

     The only recourse I *would* have is to take up the gun and try to overthrow
     the status quo.  Or threaten to do so.

  *  Some Societies/Gov'ts. may be organized on sets of underlying rules which
     are "better" - "better" because there are other alternatives besides force
     to effect "desirable" outcomes.  For example, I may consider representative
     democracy "better" because if a driving rule becomes "hoburg should work
     for free for whoever wants his labor" - maybe, just maybe - I can effect a
     change to "hoburg should also benefit from the fruits of his labor".  Most
     importantly, perhaps this can be accomplished w/o force.

     So presumably there is a rule(s) in such a society for changing the rules.
     This is what I termed a meta-rule.  Such meta-rules allow us to change
     course from "undesirable" directions to those which are more viable.
     (Presumably w/o recourse to taking up the gun.)

     Could a representative democracy not become a Libertarian society solely
     because its citizens keep voting for policies which push the society in
     that direction?

Do we allow that a Society/Gov't. which contains meta-rules for effecting
change is "better" than a closed system where the only avenue for change
is the gun?  I can always *attempt* violence to change outcomes that I feel
are intolerable (this is true for any state).  Is a state which admits some
recourse other than force for effecting change "better" than one which doesn't?

That is, are states with rules which include meta-rules-for-change "better"
than those which don't have such meta-rules?

If we allow that such a scheme *is* "better", is it reasonable to suppose that
individuals would invoke these meta-rules-for-change whenever it is in their
own self-interest to do so?  (Why should individuals act in other than their
own self-interest?)

You stated previously:

 > ...                                            The main problem is
 > that we have, as a society, accepted the idea that if we do not get
 > what we want by playing by the rules, we can always go to the
 > government and get them to change the rules in our favor.

My question is (and was), when you say this do you mean:

a)  The idea that a society should permit the rules to be changed (e.g., by
    meta-rules rather than force) is a problem, or

b)  Individuals can too easily invoke meta-rules to effect change, and *that*
    is a problem.  That is, society is *too* plastic with respect to admitting
    change.

I'm not making a judgement here, only asking what you meant.  Although if you
respond w/ b), I may ask what degree of plasticity *ought* to be desirable.

===========================================================

Off on another tangent, which is: back to the 1st question you posed at the top.

> When is it then justifiable to kill an unarmed person who is not
> initiating force?

Maybe never, but..., but...

Suppose a malignant sociopath kidnapped your family and loved ones.  Suppose
further this evil person made a very credible threat to kill them unless you
complied with a single request.  The request?  That you make your best effort
to kill one Mr. Emil Plankendorf - with your bare hands.  It is up to the
sociopath as to what constitutes a "best effort".  Unfortunately, he wants you
to work that out for yourself as you go along.

Would you have *any* moral justification for attacking Mr. Plankendorf in order
to spare your family and loved ones?

Suppose this evil person only made a very credible threat against *your* life,
unless you attack Mr. Plankendorf.  As I said, this is a credible threat, so
you have every reason to suppose this madman could and would kill you if you
fail to grant his request.

Must you simply bare your throat for the coup de grace?  Would you have *any*
justification for attacking Mr. Plankendorf in order to save your own life?

Suppose Mr. Plankendorf is reading this and knows that if you ring his doorbell,
its probably bad news for him.  Is he justified in defending himself if you
attack?  Is he justified in attacking you before you attack him?


