From: Pamela Williard and Mark Pickens <autarch@well.sf.ca.us>
Subject: PPL:  Someone born in a PPL

> The child decides that he/she dislikes the PPL community and would
> like to leave.  I would imagine that a PPL community would allow the
> child...at a certain age to leave the community.  How is this age to
> be determined?
 
> Sameer Parekh
 
The legal theory is simple.  If a person is too immature to understand his
own interests (This person need not be a child.  Maturity is NOT
age-dependent), a responsible adult, if acting as that person's guardian,
can legitmately make decisions for that person (such as where to live).  At
whatever age that person becomes mature enough to grasp the consequences of
his actions, he then becomes an adult and can ignore his former guardian's
wishes.  
 
Anyone who holds a person against his will (this includes parents) should be
prepared to defend his actions later in court.  Ultimately, the legal system
will determine whether or not the guardianship is legitimate.
 
 
 
